38
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.723
Okay, so knowing and really loving your show

39
00:01:28.723 --> 00:01:32.380
and knowing that you are a musician and you love music,

40
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.981 line:15%
and you said that you would love "New Amsterdam"

41
00:01:34.981 --> 00:01:37.361 line:15%
at one point, even if it's in your fantasy

42
00:01:37.361 --> 00:01:38.900 line:15%
to break into song.

43
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.370
What song is it?

44
00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:42.512
Oh my God, that's impossible.

45
00:01:42.512 --> 00:01:44.386
As you said that, for whatever reason,

46
00:01:44.386 --> 00:01:47.227
"I Want To Break Free", Queen, just came to mind.

47
00:01:47.227 --> 00:01:48.060
Perfect!

48
00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.070
I don't know where that fits,

49
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.240
I dunno where it makes sense, but...

50
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.462
Because it's anthemic and also,

51
00:01:53.462 --> 00:01:55.954
I think we all want to break out of something

52
00:01:55.954 --> 00:01:58.440
right now and free sounds...

53
00:01:58.440 --> 00:01:59.578
Yeah, yeah.

54
00:01:59.578 --> 00:02:01.624
But do you see life in a musical?

55
00:02:01.624 --> 00:02:04.095
I heard that you like walk around New York City

56
00:02:04.095 --> 00:02:09.095
and either listen to Bach or hear Bach in your head.

57
00:02:09.775 --> 00:02:11.730
I would say Dylan.

58
00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.300
Dylan more than Bach.

59
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:16.029
Like I remember when I first moved to New York,

60
00:02:16.029 --> 00:02:18.612
I was in a cab and I had "Fourth Time Around"

61
00:02:18.612 --> 00:02:21.300
by Bob Dylan on, from "Blonde On Blonde".

62
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.690
And I just remember watching the whole city go by

63
00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:25.070
and it was like moving there,

64
00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:26.846
and now I'm here in New York and it was just like

65
00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:30.030
that was a cool experience, that always stuck with me.

66
00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:34.818
Well, you actually have a New York experience

67
00:02:34.818 --> 00:02:39.044
story that I hearken to when I think of you,

68
00:02:39.044 --> 00:02:41.806
which is I was hanging out with you and my car got towed

69
00:02:41.806 --> 00:02:45.358
and we had to go to a Brooklyn tow yard.

70
00:02:45.358 --> 00:02:46.460
Yeah.

71
00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.875
And it was like, it felt like a musical.

72
00:02:48.875 --> 00:02:49.943
It did.

73
00:02:49.943 --> 00:02:51.804
It literally was a movie.

74
00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:53.877
There were just no cameras.

75
00:02:53.877 --> 00:02:54.710
Exactly.

76
00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:55.543
It was perfect.

77
00:02:55.543 --> 00:02:57.480
You of course, made friends with everybody

78
00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.335
who worked there who were so excited that you were there.

79
00:03:00.335 --> 00:03:02.100
It was freezing.

80
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.255
I wasn't dressed to be outside.

81
00:03:04.255 --> 00:03:05.900
I was in like, something like this

82
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.450
but it was like, you know,

83
00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:08.283
30 degrees

84
00:03:08.283 --> 00:03:09.116
28.

85
00:03:09.116 --> 00:03:10.350
Yes, exactly.

86
00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:11.240
And we were starving.

87
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:12.929
I mean, we were just completely unprepared...

88
00:03:12.929 --> 00:03:13.762
Yes! (laughs)

89
00:03:13.762 --> 00:03:14.870
For this to happen.

90
00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:16.970
I was underdressed, we didn't have any food.

91
00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:18.460
It was late.

92
00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:23.460
And we got pizza from a grocery store, from a very sweet,

93
00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:26.490
do you remember her name?

94
00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:27.570
'Cause I almost...

95
00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:28.633
She was so sweet.

96
00:03:28.633 --> 00:03:30.175
It was like Minnie.

97
00:03:30.175 --> 00:03:34.040
And yes, and she gave me crap, it was great.

98
00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:35.295
Yeah she did give you crap.

99
00:03:35.295 --> 00:03:37.192
And then we just had a slice on the curb waiting

100
00:03:37.192 --> 00:03:39.443
for your car, talking about,

101
00:03:39.443 --> 00:03:41.450
you know, Woody Allen movies and stuff.

102
00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:42.283
Yes.

103
00:03:42.283 --> 00:03:44.859
And Albert Brooks and yeah.

104
00:03:44.859 --> 00:03:48.690
Love of film and storytelling

105
00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.133
and great things being put out there.

106
00:03:51.133 --> 00:03:52.188
Yeah.

107
00:03:52.188 --> 00:03:55.286
What do you want people to take away

108
00:03:55.286 --> 00:03:59.360
from this new season of this very beloved show?

109
00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:01.330
I know your network really loves it.

110
00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:03.320
I know it really speaks to people.

111
00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:05.180
Your family is in medicine.

112
00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:07.976
The world is in a state

113
00:04:07.976 --> 00:04:11.540
that we could have never guessed it would be.

114
00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:14.250
What do you think that people can get

115
00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.540
out of your show and what is the intention behind it?

116
00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:18.721
It's not easy to do that dance

117
00:04:18.721 --> 00:04:22.325
on a medical show right now in 2020 and 2021.

118
00:04:22.325 --> 00:04:23.304
Right.

119
00:04:23.304 --> 00:04:27.179
Yeah, you know, I think the show is best.

120
00:04:27.179 --> 00:04:29.880 line:15%
I always love when the show succeeds

121
00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.872 line:15%
in tackling a macro issue like racism or climate change

122
00:04:33.872 --> 00:04:37.306 line:15%
or homelessness or something that's sort

123
00:04:37.306 --> 00:04:39.980
of seemingly unrelated to a hospital,

124
00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:41.828
that's healthcare or what,

125
00:04:41.828 --> 00:04:44.938
but certainly involves people's health

126
00:04:44.938 --> 00:04:48.100
whether it's mental or physical and why people end up

127
00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:48.933
in the hospital.

128
00:04:48.933 --> 00:04:52.510
It tackles a macro issue like that on a very micro level,

129
00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:54.590
on the individual level.

130
00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:57.560
And it's about, for me at least my character's journey

131
00:04:57.560 --> 00:05:00.470
is about acknowledging that, you know, one guy, one girl,

132
00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:03.760
one person can't change the system overnight

133
00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:05.660
by him or herself.

134
00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:08.458
But you might be able to positively impact one person's life

135
00:05:08.458 --> 00:05:11.120
or a few people or a community.

136
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:16.060
And that ripple effect is real, you know

137
00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.560
and that creates change.

138
00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:20.881
So that's, that's I think what the show does well.

139
00:05:20.881 --> 00:05:23.380
Well, I'm really glad that I asked.

140
00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.140
'Cause that was such a good answer.

141
00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.218
I'm serious.

142
00:05:26.218 --> 00:05:30.130
I'm so glad that you came, and like came in person.

143
00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:33.408
Yes, it is so, so happy to be here live.

144
00:05:33.408 --> 00:05:37.090
I've missed humanity and people

145
00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:39.599
